  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "Access Paths, Cost Model & EXPLAIN",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Heap And Index",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Heap = unordered 8 KB pages; seq scan reads all of them",
            "Selectivity of `WHERE` does not shrink a seq scan",
            "Index = separate structure mapping key values to TIDs",
            "`ctid` = block number + item offset inside that block",
            "Postgres has secondary indexes only, no InnoDB-style clustered store"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Indexes You Already Have",
          "items": [
            "One implicit index per `PRIMARY KEY`",
            "One per `UNIQUE` constraint",
            "`FOREIGN KEY` columns get none",
            "Classic cause of slow cascading deletes"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Four Access Paths"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Path",
            "Wins when"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Seq Scan",
              "most rows match"
            ],
            [
              "Index Scan",
              "few rows, random I/O"
            ],
            [
              "Index Only Scan",
              "all columns in index"
            ],
            [
              "Bitmap Index + Heap",
              "medium selectivity"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Bitmap scan builds a TID bitmap, then sorts it",
            "Heap then read in physical order, not random order",
            "Combines several indexes with bitmap AND / OR"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Cost Model"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "conf",
          "lines": [
            "seq_page_cost        = 1.0",
            "random_page_cost     = 4.0   # spinning disk",
            "cpu_tuple_cost       = 0.01",
            "cpu_index_tuple_cost = 0.005",
            "cpu_operator_cost    = 0.0025"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Planner picks by estimated cost, never by rules",
            "Index wins under roughly 5-10% row selectivity",
            "Above that, random I/O dominates and seq scan is cheaper",
            "On SSD set `random_page_cost = 1.1` or index use stays suppressed"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Where Selectivity Comes From",
          "items": [
            "`pg_statistic`, refreshed by `ANALYZE`",
            "`n_distinct`, `most_common_vals` / `freqs`",
            "`histogram_bounds` for ranges",
            "`correlation` for physical order"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Reading EXPLAIN"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)",
            "SELECT id FROM orders",
            "WHERE status = 'pending';",
            "-- rows=12 (actual rows=41000) : bad stats"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Compare `rows=` estimated against actual rows",
            "A 100x gap means stale statistics, not a missing index",
            "`Rows Removed by Filter` = index fetched rows then threw them away",
            "That index simply is not selective enough"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Index Cond, Not Filter",
          "text": "`Index Cond` is evaluated inside the index; `Filter` runs after the heap fetch. Moving a predicate from Filter to Index Cond is the whole game."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "B-tree Internals: Shape, Splits & Sibling Links",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "-- a failed build leaves an INVALID index",
            "SELECT indexrelid::regclass FROM pg_index",
            "WHERE indisvalid = false;",
            "DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx;  -- then retry"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Indexes Charge Writes",
          "text": "Every `INSERT`, `UPDATE` and `DELETE` must maintain every index on the table."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Shape Of The Tree",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Default index type in Postgres",
            "Balanced multi-way tree, all leaves at the same depth",
            "Typically 3-4 levels for hundreds of millions of rows"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flowchart",
          "spec": {
            "title": "btree index",
            "rows": [
